\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Gauge freedom of a potential}
\date{2026-11-08}


\p{The kernel in \ref{fdip-00AC} is the gauge freedom of the potential.
Adding a constant on one undirected component leaves every edge
difference unchanged. The scalar-potential contribution to the electric
field is the negative of that difference. A vector potential is assigned
to the edges, as the link
variables of lattice gauge theory are, and its field strength is the
difference of those values around a face. The identity #{d\circ d=0}
needs that second difference, from edge fields to face fields. The
maps defined here run only from vertices to edges. The vertex function
#{\chi} in the gauge transformation #{A\mapsto A+d\chi} of
[the field equations in three languages](fdip-00AA) is the other vertex
field met so far, and #{d} sends it to an edge field.}
